+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node.
+
+Lookup is served by a local embedded SQLite index file on kiosk storage. Each kiosk builds its own index from the snapshot it imports; no index content arrives over the network and no index content is shared between kiosks. Lookup opens the index read-only and resolves a serial number by exact match on an indexed column. The index file is the single artifact a lookup needs, so a lookup cannot observe a snapshot date from one import and a record from another.
+
+The index holds one record row per equipment serial number and a small set of snapshot metadata values — snapshot date, snapshot schema version, and the index layout version this build wrote. Lookup reads both the record and the snapshot date from the active index file.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
 Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
 
+Import builds the index into a new file beside the active one and activates it only after the build finishes and all validation has succeeded. Until that point the active index file is untouched and lookups continue to serve the previous snapshot. A build that fails — bad signature, unsupported schema version, duplicate serial number, exhausted disk, interrupted run — leaves the partially built file unusable to lookup and the previous index active.
+
+Serial-number uniqueness is enforced by the index itself: the serial column carries a unique constraint, so a duplicate record fails the build rather than being detected by a separate pass over the snapshot.
+
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+Normalization happens twice with one implementation: the index stores the normalized serial number in its indexed column at build time, and a lookup normalizes its argument before querying. A lookup is therefore an exact match on normalized serials, not a scan with per-row normalization, which is what keeps it inside the 250 ms goal at 90,000 records.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Index activation | Build into a new file, then swap the active file after validation succeeds | Gives REG-004's all-or-nothing activation with no partial-snapshot window, and leaves the previous index serving lookups for the whole build. |
+| Normalized serial storage | Store the normalized serial as the indexed column | An exact-match index lookup meets the 250 ms goal; normalizing per row at query time would force a scan. |
+| Snapshot metadata location | Keep snapshot date and versions inside the index file | A lookup reads the record and the snapshot date from one file, so the two cannot disagree across an import. |
+| Duplicate detection | A unique constraint on the serial column, failing the build | One mechanism enforces uniqueness for both import rejection and lookup correctness; a separate validation pass could disagree with the index. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+- Whether the source snapshot file is retained after a successful build, and what happens if the active index file is unreadable with no snapshot to rebuild from.
+- Whether import runs a free-space preflight, given that a build holds two index files and the snapshot on kiosk storage at once.
+- Whether an index whose layout version predates the installed GaugeDesk build is rebuilt, refused, or served as-is.
+- Whether lookups are available while an import is running, or the kiosk blocks them for the duration of the build.
